BirdCLEF 2023
Solution Writeup · 6th place · May 26, 2023
Thank you to the host and Kaggle for organizing the competition.
My solution is a combination of the embedding vectors of BirdNET-Analyzer V2.2 and the CNN from the BirdCLEF 2021 2nd place solution.
Model (BirdNET embedding + CNN) In order to utilize the features of BirdNET, the embedding of BirdNET is concatenated to the output of the CNN of the BirdCLEF 2021 2nd place solution.
BirdNET embedding The embedding vectors of BirdNET were created with BirdNET V2.2 embeddings.py. I modified BirdNET V2.2 audio.py so that if the length of the audio data is shorter than BirdNET's sample rate (48000), the data is padded to output at least 1 second of embedding vector. Using V2.2 rather than the latest version BirdNET V2.3, slightly improved cv.
CNN The backbone of the CNN from the BirdCLEF 2021 2nd place solution used timm's eca_nfnet_l1 and seresnext26t_32x4d.
Training After pretraining with data from BirdCLEF 2021 + BirdCLEF 2022, I train the CNN and other linear layers with data from BirdCLEF 2023. BirdNET is not trained.
The input for training is data for 30 seconds. Since BirdNET outputs embedding vectors for 3 seconds of data, I averaged each of the embedding vectors for 30 seconds.
In most experiments, cv was highest in the final epoch, so I included all data in the training set.
The main training parameters are as follows:
loss: BCEWithLogitsLoss
MelSpectrogram
sample_rate: 32000, window_size: 1024, hop_size: 320, fmin: 0, fmax: 14000, mel_bins: 128, power: 2, top_db=None labels: primary label=0.9995, secondary label=0.4 or 0.5 (BirdCLEF 2022 3rd place solution), label smoothing=0.01
cv: primary_label StratifiedKFold(n_splits=5)
optimizer: AdamW(weight_decay=1e-4)
scheduler: warmup 0-3epoch(lr=3e-6->3e-4) + Cosine Annealing 3-70epoch(lr=3e-4->3e-6)
Augmentation As in the previous two competitions, augmentation was important. I combined the following augmentations:
audiomentations.Shift Mixup (BirdCLEF 2021 2nd place solution) Gaussian Noise random lowpass filter random power (BirdCLEF 2021 5th place solution) torchaudio.transforms.FrequencyMasking torchaudio.transforms.TimeMasking Oversampling The following Oversampling was performed, but I think the effect was minimal.
Oversampling to have at least 20 of data for each class
I doubled the number of training data that have two or more secondary labels because data with more than two secondary_labels have worse val_loss
As in the BirdCLEF 2022 5th place solution, files with reverb/echo effects created for some minority class data and added to the training data
Strong augmentation was applied to the oversampled data
Inference The CNN input is given 5 seconds of data for inference, and the BirdNET input is given the first 3 seconds of data for inference.
To submit within 2 hours, I converted each model to ONNX
As in this notebook, I also used ThreadPoolExecutor to speed up inference
Ensembling The outputs of 2 models with different CNN backbones (eca_nfnet_l1, seresnext26t_32x4d) simply averaged.
What did not work add background noise (BirdCLEF 2021 2nd place solution) postprocess (BirdCLEF 2022 2nd place solution) Speeding up inference using OpenVINO Even if the model was converted to OpenVINO, the inference time was not much different from ONNX. I think I just used it wrong. Using MFCC as input (as in this paper) Using CQT as input Using FAMILY etc. from eBird_Taxonomy_v2021.csv for prediction (as in this paper) Rough lb history name Private Score Public Score BirdCLEF 2021 2nd place solution CNN (with BirdCLEF 2021 + BirdCLEF 2022 pretrain) 0.71 0.80 BirdCLEF 2021 2nd place solution CNN + Augmentation + BirdNET embedding 0.74 0.82 BirdCLEF 2021 2nd place solution CNN + Augmentation + BirdNET embedding + Oversampling + Ensembling 0.75 0.83 Author anonamename anonamename
